"""Writing Skills: prompts (<=300 words), approach, and model responses."""
W = [
 dict(id="W1", prompt="Some mistakes teach us more than any success. Others we would give anything to undo. Reflect on the idea that failure and regret can shape who we become.",
  plan=["Open with one concrete moment (a mistake you made or saw).", "Say what it cost and what it taught – be specific, not general.", "Contrast ‘regret that paralyses’ with ‘regret that teaches’.", "Close by linking the lesson to who you are trying to become."],
  model="In Class 9 I copied a friend's science homework. I told myself it was harmless; the teacher spotted it within a minute, and what stung was not the zero but the look of disappointment on my friend's face. He had trusted me with his work.<br><br>For weeks I wished I could rewind that morning. Regret, I learned, comes in two kinds. One kind keeps you replaying the moment until it shrinks you; the other kind asks, ‘What will I do differently?’ The first is a prison, the second a teacher.<br><br>I chose, slowly, the second. I apologised, redid the assignment, and started a habit I still keep: beginning homework on the day it is given, so that panic never gets to choose for me. Last year, when a classmate asked to copy my notes the night before a test, I said no – and offered to study with him instead. We both passed.<br><br>I still would not mind if I could undo that Tuesday. But I have stopped wishing it away, because the person who refuses shortcuts today was made, in part, by the person who took one. Mistakes cannot be rewound, but they can be put to work."),
 dict(id="W2", prompt="A place or a smell can carry you back to a moment you wish you could live again. Write about an experience you would happily repeat – and why it matters to you.",
  plan=["Start with the sensory detail (smell, sound, light).", "Narrate the experience in 2–3 vivid lines, not a list.", "Ask: why do I want it again – what did it give me?", "End with what you now do to keep that feeling alive."],
  model="The smell of wet earth after the first monsoon shower takes me, at once, to my grandmother's courtyard. I am nine; the ground steams, the neem leaves drip, and she has placed a steel plate of puffed rice and hot tea at the edge of the verandah because, she says, ‘rain tastes better with something to eat’.<br><br>Nothing remarkable happened that afternoon. We simply sat. She told me how she crossed a flooded field to school, and I told her about a cartoon. Yet I would give much to repeat it, because it was the last time I did nothing and felt that nothing was enough.<br><br>Today my afternoons are timetables. I look forward to holidays the way a runner looks at a finishing line. That afternoon reminds me that happiness was never in the event; it was in the attention. So when it rains now, I switch off my phone for ten minutes, open the window and breathe in. It is not the courtyard, but for ten minutes it is close – and I have learned that repeating a moment sometimes means recreating its stillness, not its setting."),
 dict(id="W3", prompt="‘We do not always get a second chance.’ Reflect on a decision that could not be undone and on what you would keep, even if time could be rewound.",
  plan=["Name the decision and why it was final.", "Show the two sides: what you lost and what you gained.", "State what you would keep even if you could rewind.", "Close with a principle for future choices."],
  model="Two years ago I left my school's football team to concentrate on exams. The decision was final: the squad that year went on to win the district cup, and my place was filled by someone else.<br><br>For a while I felt I had made a poor trade. Every Saturday I heard the cheering from the ground and told myself the results would justify it. Then they did – partly. My marks improved; my friendships on the team faded.<br><br>If I could rewind, would I choose differently? I think I would keep the decision but change the way I made it. I decided alone, in one evening, on the basis of fear. A conversation with my coach might have shown me a way to do both. What I would keep is the courage to put a long-term goal ahead of an immediate pleasure; what I would change is the habit of deciding in silence.<br><br>That is the rule I use now: for any choice that cannot be undone, I sleep on it and ask one person who knows me well. Second chances are rare, but second opinions are free."),
]
